  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Absent Presences in the Colonial Archive is showing an early but meaningful scholarly and usage footprint. The book has been downloaded 1,945 times through OAPEN, and it has already received 3 citations in OpenAlex, indicating that it is being read and taken up within related research communities.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Irene Hilden\u2019s book, published by Leuven University Press in 2022, examines the Berlin Sound Archive through a postcolonial lens, centring the colonial conditions under which its recordings were produced. Its conceptual profile and abstract point to a work situated at the intersection of colonial history, sound studies, ethnography, narrative, and active listening, with clear relevance to scholarship on archives, institutions, and power.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The strongest evidence of reach is usage: 1,945 OAPEN downloads suggest sustained interest in the book as an open-access resource. That pattern is complemented by a hybrid open-access record, with multiple repository instances indexed in OpenAIRE, which likely supports discoverability and reuse across academic settings.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The citation record is modest but already visible. Three citing works appeared in 2024 and 2025, mainly in musicological and adjacent humanities venues, including The World of Music and Ars digitalis. This pattern suggests that the book is beginning to inform specialist discussions, especially around the Berlin Phonogramm-Archiv, digitization, repatriation, and the ethics of archival access.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The surrounding record also points to institutional and collaborative grounding: funding is associated with KU Leuven, Humboldt-Universit\u00e4t zu Berlin, and CARMAH. At the same time, the evidence base is still relatively small, so the record should be read as an emerging impact profile rather than a mature one. What is clear is that the book is circulating, being read, and beginning to shape scholarly conversation in its field.</p>",
